From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: map IMEM as device memory, not normal memory

ipa_imem_init() maps the IPA's window into IMEM with IOMMU_READ|IOMMU_WRITE,
which leaves the SMMU page table entry with Normal cacheable attributes. IMEM
is not DRAM: it is an on-chip SRAM slave, and on SM6375 issuing cacheable
transactions at it is not merely inefficient, it is fatal. The SoC resets
instantly with no CPU exception, no panic and not one character of console
output — the same failure mode this port already hit once when the IPA shared
memory window was pointed at a hole (see HANDOFF-SESSION4.md §1).

The symptom is a watchdog reset a few minutes after the modem registers on a
network, and only then: it needs the modem to be actually using the IPA, so it
never appeared while the modem was stuck offline. With the modem's radio off,
or with ipa.ko not loaded, the device runs indefinitely.

The downstream Motorola SM6375 kernel maps exactly this region with the device
attribute, in the code that consumes the `qcom,additional-mapping` property
that carries it:

	techpack/dataipa/.../ipa_v3/ipa.c:8951
		ipa3_iommu_map(cb->iommu_domain,
			rounddown(iova, PAGE_SIZE), rounddown(pa, PAGE_SIZE),
			roundup(size + pa - rounddown(pa, PAGE_SIZE), PAGE_SIZE),
			IOMMU_READ | IOMMU_WRITE | IOMMU_MMIO);

	blair.dtsi, ipa_smmu_ap:
		qcom,additional-mapping =
		/* modem tables in IMEM */
		<0x0C123000 0x0C123000 0x2000>;

IOMMU_MMIO is the flag that makes the SMMU use Device-nGnRE for the entry, and
it is the correct description of the slave on every SoC, not just this one:
IMEM is memory-mapped I/O, and no user of this mapping wants it cached.

--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_mem.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_mem.c
@@ -472,8 +472,12 @@
 	size = PAGE_ALIGN(size + addr - phys);
 	iova = phys;	/* We just want a direct mapping */
 
-	ret = iommu_map(domain, iova, phys, size, IOMMU_READ | IOMMU_WRITE,
-			GFP_KERNEL);
+	/* IMEM is an on-chip SRAM slave, not DRAM. It must be mapped as device
+	 * memory: on SM6375 a cacheable mapping makes the SoC reset without a
+	 * trace as soon as the IPA touches it.
+	 */
+	ret = iommu_map(domain, iova, phys, size,
+			IOMMU_READ | IOMMU_WRITE | IOMMU_MMIO, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
